WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.718 --> 00:00:02.309
- When you get on the field,

00:00:02.309 --> 00:00:05.242
put everything you have into it.

00:00:05.242 --> 00:00:08.441
Put every ounce of energy
you have on the field.

00:00:08.441 --> 00:00:12.622
Fear is the disease,
hustle is the antidote.

00:00:12.622 --> 00:00:14.317
What I do in a given day is I have

00:00:14.317 --> 00:00:17.743
a set of problems that need to be solved,

00:00:17.743 --> 00:00:20.852
and I start at the most
important and work my way down.

00:00:20.852 --> 00:00:22.782
- He's an American
visionary and entrepreneur.

00:00:22.782 --> 00:00:26.048
He co-founded the peer
to peer file sharing

00:00:26.048 --> 00:00:28.891
company Swoosh, and the
technology company Uber.

00:00:28.891 --> 00:00:32.124
He has an estimated net
worth of $6.2 billion.

00:00:32.124 --> 00:00:34.383
He's Travis Kalanick, and here is my take

00:00:34.383 --> 00:00:36.330
on his top 10 rules for success.

00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:37.694
Rule number one is my personal favorite,

00:00:37.694 --> 00:00:39.365
and make sure to stick around all the way

00:00:39.365 --> 00:00:41.115
to the end for some special bonus clips.

00:00:41.115 --> 00:00:42.707
And as always, if Travis
says something that

00:00:42.707 --> 00:00:45.132
really means something,
really resonates with you,

00:00:45.132 --> 00:00:47.331
make sure to leave it in
the comments and put quotes

00:00:47.331 --> 00:00:51.498
around it so other people
can be inspired as well.

00:00:54.152 --> 00:00:57.319
(inspirational music)

00:01:03.435 --> 00:01:05.632
- The champion's mindset, I
think a lot of people hear

00:01:05.632 --> 00:01:09.337
the word champion and they
think of a famous sports star,

00:01:09.337 --> 00:01:13.247
in the US you might think of
Michael Jordan or Lebron James

00:01:13.247 --> 00:01:15.970
going and dunking on somebody.

00:01:15.970 --> 00:01:18.540
But we think of it differently.

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:22.328
We think of it as, well
when you get on the field,

00:01:22.328 --> 00:01:25.468
put everything you have into it.

00:01:25.468 --> 00:01:28.407
Put every ounce of energy
you have on the field.

00:01:28.407 --> 00:01:30.895
Leave nothing on the field.

00:01:30.895 --> 00:01:34.175
And two, when you get knocked down,

00:01:34.175 --> 00:01:38.342
'cause if you're an entrepreneur,
inevitably you will be,

00:01:40.457 --> 00:01:42.311
when you see hard times
and you get knocked down,

00:01:42.311 --> 00:01:43.311
get back up.

00:01:44.648 --> 00:01:47.802
And if you keep putting
everything you got into it,

00:01:47.802 --> 00:01:50.650
and you keep getting back up
when you get knocked down,

00:01:50.650 --> 00:01:54.050
it's almost impossible to fail.

00:01:54.050 --> 00:01:57.717
Fear is the disease,
hustle is the antidote.

00:01:58.756 --> 00:02:02.891
So in my day, I already sound
like an old-school dude,

00:02:02.891 --> 00:02:06.460
in my day, it as just tough, man.

00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:10.670
You start a company in
2001, good luck, right?

00:02:10.670 --> 00:02:12.370
You can't count on funding,

00:02:12.370 --> 00:02:13.809
you can't count on sales,

00:02:13.809 --> 00:02:16.919
you can't count on anything
but just crazy hustle.

00:02:16.919 --> 00:02:19.036
And just grit your teeth,

00:02:19.036 --> 00:02:21.119
claw your way to success.

00:02:23.134 --> 00:02:24.928
There was just no easy way to do it.

00:02:24.928 --> 00:02:28.291
What I find is a lot of
the entrepreneurs today

00:02:28.291 --> 00:02:31.592
are much smarter than
we were back in the day.

00:02:31.592 --> 00:02:33.308
They're like way smarter.

00:02:33.308 --> 00:02:35.678
They got blogs, they
know what VCs are doing,

00:02:35.678 --> 00:02:39.312
they know everything that's
going on in the space.

00:02:39.312 --> 00:02:41.600
There's a corpus of knowledge now that

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:45.364
makes entrepreneurs today much smarter.

00:02:45.364 --> 00:02:47.558
But there's liquidity in funding,

00:02:47.558 --> 00:02:49.072
especially on the angel side.

00:02:49.072 --> 00:02:51.068
It used to be much harder
to get an angel deal done

00:02:51.068 --> 00:02:52.189
and a series A deal.

00:02:52.189 --> 00:02:55.199
Now, angels deals are a piece of cake.

00:02:55.199 --> 00:02:58.660
But what happens is, I see
a number of entrepreneurs

00:02:58.660 --> 00:03:00.910
who don't have that hustle.

00:03:02.667 --> 00:03:05.637
And they're afraid, and
they act out of that fear.

00:03:05.637 --> 00:03:06.938
And when you act out of that fear,

00:03:06.938 --> 00:03:08.656
you make big mistakes.

00:03:08.656 --> 00:03:11.595
An example might be doing a
preemptive deal with a VC,

00:03:11.595 --> 00:03:13.185
thinking they're your friend.

00:03:13.185 --> 00:03:17.270
An example might be not being
hardcore and keeping your job.

00:03:17.270 --> 00:03:20.187
An example might be, a lot of times

00:03:21.633 --> 00:03:23.599
it gets exposed through deals.

00:03:23.599 --> 00:03:26.860
You see that fear being
exposed through the deal cycle.

00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:30.325
And so if you're an entrepreneur

00:03:30.325 --> 00:03:33.668
and you find yourself afraid of something,

00:03:33.668 --> 00:03:34.835
sack up, okay?

00:03:37.052 --> 00:03:38.635
And start hustling.

00:03:39.608 --> 00:03:42.608
Hustling is the antidote to fear.

00:03:42.608 --> 00:03:44.525
Go create your success.

00:03:45.362 --> 00:03:50.040
My management style is
problem solver in chief.

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:52.873
And so what I do in a given day is

00:03:54.174 --> 00:03:55.630
I have a set of problems
that need to be solved,

00:03:55.630 --> 00:04:00.270
and I start at the most
important and work my way down.

00:04:00.270 --> 00:04:02.682
And for the problems
I don't have time for,

00:04:02.682 --> 00:04:04.693
Somebody else gets to solve them.

00:04:04.693 --> 00:04:06.780
If somebody's already solving a problem,

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:08.961
I don't need to get involved.

00:04:08.961 --> 00:04:10.958
The problem's getting solved.

00:04:10.958 --> 00:04:14.329
But people go, "Wow, so your
day's just full of problems."

00:04:14.329 --> 00:04:16.556
I'm like, yeah, I got a lot of problems!

00:04:16.556 --> 00:04:20.723
But it's sort of how a math
professor thinks of problems.

00:04:22.914 --> 00:04:26.221
Think of a great math professor who has

00:04:26.221 --> 00:04:28.176
no math problems to solve.

00:04:28.176 --> 00:04:29.906
He's a very sad math professor.

00:04:29.906 --> 00:04:31.372
(audience laughing)

00:04:31.372 --> 00:04:34.872
And so, that's very much how I look at it.

00:04:35.956 --> 00:04:39.261
Solving problems, every
problem is an opportunity

00:04:39.261 --> 00:04:42.185
for innovating and being creative,

00:04:42.185 --> 00:04:43.794
and finding something new.

00:04:43.794 --> 00:04:46.684
And that's what I love to
do, and I do it every day.

00:04:46.684 --> 00:04:48.656
A lot of folks think,
oh you're an engineer,

00:04:48.656 --> 00:04:51.097
you're analytical, you can
solve all the world's problems.

00:04:51.097 --> 00:04:53.315
But it doesn't quite work like that.

00:04:53.315 --> 00:04:56.040
There's a lot of creativity
that goes into finding

00:04:56.040 --> 00:04:59.843
how to solve a problem in
a way that moves people.

00:04:59.843 --> 00:05:02.413
In a way that inspires.

00:05:02.413 --> 00:05:06.571
And so you have to really
merge the left brain,

00:05:06.571 --> 00:05:08.439
that analytical side,
with the right brain,

00:05:08.439 --> 00:05:10.404
which is the creative
side, the emotional side,

00:05:10.404 --> 00:05:13.982
the heart, and put those two together.

00:05:13.982 --> 00:05:15.615
This is the Babbage machine,

00:05:15.615 --> 00:05:18.060
actually the first computing
machine that existed,

00:05:18.060 --> 00:05:20.675
or actually that was designed.

00:05:20.675 --> 00:05:23.429
Super-analytical, but what the hell

00:05:23.429 --> 00:05:25.731
are you going to do with it?

00:05:25.731 --> 00:05:26.891
Right?

00:05:26.891 --> 00:05:28.453
You have to bring some creativity to it,

00:05:28.453 --> 00:05:30.462
and Albert Einstein, I've got a couple of

00:05:30.462 --> 00:05:32.583
Albert Einstein quotes today, says,

00:05:32.583 --> 00:05:35.770
"Creativity is intelligence having fun."

00:05:35.770 --> 00:05:38.503
So at Uber, whether it's
myself as the entrepreneur

00:05:38.503 --> 00:05:39.819
or really anybody we hire,

00:05:39.819 --> 00:05:43.008
we really look for that
analytical-creative cross.

00:05:43.008 --> 00:05:45.881
DC, we had a really interesting situation.

00:05:45.881 --> 00:05:49.172
We went there, by the way,
as far as we could tell,

00:05:49.172 --> 00:05:51.504
we were totally legal, white glove legal,

00:05:51.504 --> 00:05:55.960
nicest laws in the country
in terms of sedans in DC.

00:05:55.960 --> 00:05:59.284
But DC taxi commissioner
goes out there and says,

00:05:59.284 --> 00:06:01.703
Uber is not legal because

00:06:01.703 --> 00:06:03.275
they charge by time and distance.

00:06:03.275 --> 00:06:05.086
And let's just say that was real.

00:06:05.086 --> 00:06:08.163
Why is charging by distance evil?

00:06:08.163 --> 00:06:11.659
I don't understand. I don't get it.

00:06:11.659 --> 00:06:12.696
But he said, "Look, you're charging by

00:06:12.696 --> 00:06:13.975
time and distance, you're not allowed."

00:06:13.975 --> 00:06:16.290
Well, we looked at the law, the law says,

00:06:16.290 --> 00:06:18.114
"Sedan, a for-hire
vehicle designed to carry

00:06:18.114 --> 00:06:19.970
fewer than six passengers,
which charges for service

00:06:19.970 --> 00:06:22.877
on the basis of time and mileage."

00:06:22.877 --> 00:06:23.791
Like what are you talking about?

00:06:23.791 --> 00:06:26.038
He's going in public forums,
Washington Post, etc.

00:06:26.038 --> 00:06:30.826
We go to the Attorney General
in the District of Columbia,

00:06:30.826 --> 00:06:32.155
and that's what he tells us.

00:06:32.155 --> 00:06:35.322
(audience applauding)

00:06:36.390 --> 00:06:39.965
So, when you read about the crazy stuff

00:06:39.965 --> 00:06:41.278
we're doing in the cities,

00:06:41.278 --> 00:06:42.456
and I've got to close this down 'cause

00:06:42.456 --> 00:06:44.015
somebody's going to have the hook,

00:06:44.015 --> 00:06:45.347
and they're going to take me off here.

00:06:45.347 --> 00:06:47.373
But when you read about the crazy stuff

00:06:47.373 --> 00:06:48.624
that we're doing in the cities,

00:06:48.624 --> 00:06:51.457
know that it is corrupt out there.

00:06:53.311 --> 00:06:56.110
Know that we are highly,
highly disruptive,

00:06:56.110 --> 00:06:59.063
and what you read in the
papers isn't always true.

00:06:59.063 --> 00:07:03.416
And the bottom line is that in
order to be in this business,

00:07:03.416 --> 00:07:06.538
in order to be this
disruptive to what's going on,

00:07:06.538 --> 00:07:09.205
you have to be willing to fight.

00:07:11.871 --> 00:07:13.868
And you have to not be-- you can't be shy.

00:07:13.868 --> 00:07:16.931
So that gives you a little bit about it.

00:07:16.931 --> 00:07:19.199
Basically, they tried to
put a floor in our prices,

00:07:19.199 --> 00:07:20.855
I'll leave it at this
one, this last one here.

00:07:20.855 --> 00:07:22.911
They put a floor in our prices,

00:07:22.911 --> 00:07:25.760
or tried to pass what they
called "The Uber Amendment."

00:07:25.760 --> 00:07:29.007
Make our prices five times that of a taxi.

00:07:29.007 --> 00:07:32.360
They rolled the amendment out July 10th,

00:07:32.360 --> 00:07:35.856
it was a Monday, sorry July 9th.

00:07:35.856 --> 00:07:37.883
The vote on the bill was the 10th--

00:07:37.883 --> 00:07:41.159
Sorry, they put the bill out on the 9th,

00:07:41.159 --> 00:07:42.631
that was a Monday, at 4:00 p.m.

00:07:42.631 --> 00:07:44.562
to vote on at 11:00 a.m. the next day.

00:07:44.562 --> 00:07:46.851
18 hours, most of which
you're going to be sleeping.

00:07:46.851 --> 00:07:48.119
I wrote an e-mail to our consumers

00:07:48.119 --> 00:07:51.228
letting them know that
they're about to do this.

00:07:51.228 --> 00:07:52.884
And by the way, the rationale was

00:07:52.884 --> 00:07:55.171
to ensure that basically we don't compete.

00:07:55.171 --> 00:07:57.971
If a CEO of a company
said something like this,

00:07:57.971 --> 00:07:59.330
they'd be in jail.

00:07:59.330 --> 00:08:01.219
But if you corrupt your politicians

00:08:01.219 --> 00:08:04.418
and then push those laws
down, it's totally legal.

00:08:04.418 --> 00:08:06.029
Anyway, we did something called

00:08:06.029 --> 00:08:08.393
"Life, Liberty, and the
Pursuit of Uberness",

00:08:08.393 --> 00:08:10.421
UberDCLove was the hashtag.

00:08:10.421 --> 00:08:13.852
18 hours later, we had
50,000 original e-mails,

00:08:13.852 --> 00:08:15.319
these weren't robo e-mails,

00:08:15.319 --> 00:08:17.144
that went to City Council people

00:08:17.144 --> 00:08:18.647
telling them not to vote for it.

00:08:18.647 --> 00:08:23.394
37,000 tweets, 104 million
social media impressions,

00:08:23.394 --> 00:08:24.311
and we won.

00:08:25.820 --> 00:08:28.987
(audience applauding)

00:08:34.744 --> 00:08:35.703
As you might imagine, there wasn't

00:08:35.703 --> 00:08:37.623
a lot of sleep during that time.

00:08:37.623 --> 00:08:40.018
But it was so short it
maybe didn't matter.

00:08:40.018 --> 00:08:41.768
Number 6, make magic.

00:08:42.603 --> 00:08:46.330
So I'm a little old-school
in my movie taste.

00:08:46.330 --> 00:08:48.571
There's a movie called Pulp Fiction,

00:08:48.571 --> 00:08:50.318
where Samuel L. Jackson, there's this

00:08:50.318 --> 00:08:51.819
glowing thing in this briefcase

00:08:51.819 --> 00:08:52.979
and you never quite see it,

00:08:52.979 --> 00:08:55.268
but everybody, when they're around it,

00:08:55.268 --> 00:08:57.310
they're close to it, their eyes get big

00:08:57.310 --> 00:09:00.294
and they're just excited,
they just feel it,

00:09:00.294 --> 00:09:03.281
they feel that aura of it.

00:09:03.281 --> 00:09:05.277
How do you recreate that
in the things we make?

00:09:05.277 --> 00:09:07.874
The things entrepreneurs make,
how do they recreate that?

00:09:07.874 --> 00:09:10.765
Well, at Uber, we like to say

00:09:10.765 --> 00:09:13.535
there are four dimensions of magic.

00:09:13.535 --> 00:09:17.183
One is time, if you can
give people their time back,

00:09:17.183 --> 00:09:21.350
you are providing some kind
of magic to your customers.

00:09:23.665 --> 00:09:26.960
If you can bring calm into their day,

00:09:26.960 --> 00:09:30.206
you are very valuable to the folks

00:09:30.206 --> 00:09:32.480
who are using your products.

00:09:32.480 --> 00:09:37.196
Or sometimes, it's just
bringing a little bit of joy.

00:09:37.196 --> 00:09:39.884
And for others, it's money.

00:09:39.884 --> 00:09:42.301
So time, calm, joy, and money

00:09:45.051 --> 00:09:48.716
are at Uber the four dimensions of magic.

00:09:48.716 --> 00:09:50.477
Money can be saving people money,

00:09:50.477 --> 00:09:52.967
so they get much more
than what they expected.

00:09:52.967 --> 00:09:56.367
Or it can be new ways to
make an income that changed

00:09:56.367 --> 00:10:00.534
your life and changed how
you provide for your family.

00:10:04.623 --> 00:10:07.123
And so, magic often, you know,

00:10:08.429 --> 00:10:09.776
you kind of know it when you see it.

00:10:09.776 --> 00:10:10.964
I mean, this is amazing.

00:10:10.964 --> 00:10:14.214
This is a patent on the iPhone in 2006.

00:10:15.868 --> 00:10:17.276
And I'm looking at my iPhone,

00:10:17.276 --> 00:10:20.518
I'm like, I think it's kind of the same.

00:10:20.518 --> 00:10:24.232
Sometimes it requires seeing the future.

00:10:24.232 --> 00:10:27.231
But you ultimately know
it when you see it.

00:10:27.231 --> 00:10:30.558
These are two bridges in San Francisco.

00:10:30.558 --> 00:10:32.808
They both go into the city.

00:10:33.743 --> 00:10:34.950
One is the Bay Bridge and one is

00:10:34.950 --> 00:10:36.267
of course the Golden Gate Bridge.

00:10:36.267 --> 00:10:37.472
And no matter how many times

00:10:37.472 --> 00:10:38.847
you go on the Golden Gate Bridge,

00:10:38.847 --> 00:10:40.175
you're going to take a picture.

00:10:40.175 --> 00:10:41.367
You just can't help it.

00:10:41.367 --> 00:10:43.795
And so when you see magic, you know it.

00:10:43.795 --> 00:10:47.138
It inspires, it can be
aesthetic on the outside,

00:10:47.138 --> 00:10:49.671
but sometimes you can't see it.

00:10:49.671 --> 00:10:50.504
As an entrepreneur, there actually

00:10:50.504 --> 00:10:52.450
is a definition of failure.

00:10:52.450 --> 00:10:54.225
A lot of people ask me,
when do you move on?

00:10:54.225 --> 00:10:56.415
When do you stop what you're doing

00:10:56.415 --> 00:11:00.020
and say maybe this isn't working out?

00:11:00.020 --> 00:11:03.052
And I like to say, well first
you have to believe in--

00:11:03.052 --> 00:11:05.944
If you keep believing in what
you're doing, keep going.

00:11:05.944 --> 00:11:07.320
But there's one other criteria.

00:11:07.320 --> 00:11:11.487
If keeping going means you are
going to do severe physical

00:11:12.424 --> 00:11:16.519
or mental damage to yourself,
then you should move on.

00:11:16.519 --> 00:11:18.251
- [Interviewer] Yeah.

00:11:18.251 --> 00:11:21.020
- And my last company, I
sort of got to that point.

00:11:21.020 --> 00:11:24.608
It was a success for
me, I ultimately sold it

00:11:24.608 --> 00:11:29.081
to a large company out
of Boston called Akamai.

00:11:29.081 --> 00:11:33.948
But I was at that point
where I'd gone seven years,

00:11:33.948 --> 00:11:35.680
and it was really hard times,

00:11:35.680 --> 00:11:37.908
and I knew that if I
had kept going in this

00:11:37.908 --> 00:11:41.109
little blip of success
we had started to see,

00:11:41.109 --> 00:11:43.289
if that was a mirage, I knew I would

00:11:43.289 --> 00:11:46.039
start to go crazy, like for real.

00:11:47.310 --> 00:11:49.310
Failure is maybe when...

00:11:50.421 --> 00:11:51.922
At least I feel like I failed when

00:11:51.922 --> 00:11:55.088
I didn't put everything
I had into a problem.

00:11:55.088 --> 00:11:56.404
So really, it goes back to

00:11:56.404 --> 00:11:58.753
the analogy I made at the beginning.

00:11:58.753 --> 00:12:00.871
If you don't put it all out there,

00:12:00.871 --> 00:12:02.388
if you don't put everything you got

00:12:02.388 --> 00:12:05.031
into solving the problem,
then that's failure.

00:12:05.031 --> 00:12:07.742
If you get to a place where
it's easy as an entrepreneur,

00:12:07.742 --> 00:12:11.675
it's about to get really, really hard.

00:12:11.675 --> 00:12:14.092
And so, what you find is with

00:12:15.601 --> 00:12:18.615
really good entrepreneurs,
they're constantly

00:12:18.615 --> 00:12:21.660
making things difficult for themselves.

00:12:21.660 --> 00:12:25.667
And I like to say you push until it hurts.

00:12:25.667 --> 00:12:29.535
Or you want to be always pushing beyond

00:12:29.535 --> 00:12:31.279
what you're comfortable with and

00:12:31.279 --> 00:12:35.066
you're sweating just a
little bit all the time.

00:12:35.066 --> 00:12:37.434
And when that happens,
when you're constantly

00:12:37.434 --> 00:12:41.640
pushing beyond what you know is possible,

00:12:41.640 --> 00:12:42.971
you're always sweating a little bit,

00:12:42.971 --> 00:12:45.382
and you're always a little bit nervous,

00:12:45.382 --> 00:12:48.755
but that is the drug of
being an entrepreneur.

00:12:48.755 --> 00:12:50.875
And so, when you're pushing like that,

00:12:50.875 --> 00:12:53.054
and you're never really relaxed,

00:12:53.054 --> 00:12:55.577
then it always feels small.

00:12:55.577 --> 00:12:57.695
And so in many ways it feels to me

00:12:57.695 --> 00:13:01.606
the same now as it did at the beginning.

00:13:01.606 --> 00:13:03.356
I'm still a little nervous.

00:13:03.356 --> 00:13:05.831
No, look, sometimes you talk to people

00:13:05.831 --> 00:13:08.211
in the entrepreneurial world,

00:13:08.211 --> 00:13:10.037
and they're like, "Man, I'm
looking for my next big thing,

00:13:10.037 --> 00:13:12.537
I got this idea, I'm kind of--

00:13:13.475 --> 00:13:16.565
You know, it's like eCommerce
crossed with social,

00:13:16.565 --> 00:13:19.425
but then it's got this mobile twist."

00:13:19.425 --> 00:13:21.001
(audience laughing)

00:13:21.001 --> 00:13:25.550
And you're like, "What are
you talking about, man?"

00:13:25.550 --> 00:13:29.743
So the best way to do this, and this is--

00:13:29.743 --> 00:13:32.527
look I'm biased, this
is my way of doing it,

00:13:32.527 --> 00:13:34.689
I think the best way,

00:13:34.689 --> 00:13:37.106
is fall in love with an idea.

00:13:39.234 --> 00:13:43.675
Fall in love with an idea
and just go after it.

00:13:43.675 --> 00:13:46.508
And when you do that, win or lose,

00:13:47.942 --> 00:13:51.113
get knocked down, get back up.

00:13:51.113 --> 00:13:54.034
Whatever happens, if you
put your all into it,

00:13:54.034 --> 00:13:56.284
it's a worthwhile endeavor.

00:13:57.127 --> 00:13:58.428
And you got to get smarter every day,

00:13:58.428 --> 00:14:00.561
because what you fall in
love with might not be

00:14:00.561 --> 00:14:03.805
exactly what it needs to be to win.

00:14:03.805 --> 00:14:07.767
But start there, and
then the journey becomes

00:14:07.767 --> 00:14:11.478
something worthwhile, because
it's going to be hard,

00:14:11.478 --> 00:14:15.161
and if you've got love,
then it's much easier

00:14:15.161 --> 00:14:18.434
than if you don't, much easier.

00:14:18.434 --> 00:14:20.131
The entrepreneur that loves what they do

00:14:20.131 --> 00:14:23.398
going against the
entrepreneur that doesn't, oh!

00:14:23.398 --> 00:14:25.669
Really obvious who wins that one,

00:14:25.669 --> 00:14:27.216
not even a close call.

00:14:27.216 --> 00:14:29.360
Now, when I was an
engineer, I used to think

00:14:29.360 --> 00:14:32.048
that selling was like
this super terrible thing,

00:14:32.048 --> 00:14:35.524
like I used to judge it
like all engineers do.

00:14:35.524 --> 00:14:38.449
And I haven't always been the
greatest storyteller myself,

00:14:38.449 --> 00:14:41.554
I had to sort of get up
to speed a little bit,

00:14:41.554 --> 00:14:43.427
because I thought if you're
just telling a story,

00:14:43.427 --> 00:14:46.177
you look something like this guy.

00:14:47.879 --> 00:14:49.364
But we have fun when we story-tell,

00:14:49.364 --> 00:14:51.127
we like to say that transportation is

00:14:51.127 --> 00:14:53.627
kind of a boring old business.

00:14:55.131 --> 00:14:58.024
Can we find ways to bring it to life?

00:14:58.024 --> 00:15:00.033
Can we give our customers who love us

00:15:00.033 --> 00:15:02.616
a reason to tell their friends?

00:15:03.790 --> 00:15:06.185
One of the first
on-demand marketing stunts

00:15:06.185 --> 00:15:08.384
that we did was on-demand mariachi bands

00:15:08.384 --> 00:15:10.807
on the 5th of May, Cinco de Mayo.

00:15:10.807 --> 00:15:13.048
So you pushed a button
and these guys show up,

00:15:13.048 --> 00:15:16.599
and everybody in the office was like,

00:15:16.599 --> 00:15:18.266
"What is happening?"

00:15:20.654 --> 00:15:22.745
And our India team, they had this idea

00:15:22.745 --> 00:15:25.015
that they would do on-demand choppers.

00:15:25.015 --> 00:15:27.601
This was definitely a
one-time event. (laughs)

00:15:27.601 --> 00:15:31.001
(audience laughing)

00:15:31.001 --> 00:15:33.611
But you can go to the next
level in storytelling.

00:15:33.611 --> 00:15:37.014
You can go to a place where you're

00:15:37.014 --> 00:15:39.440
really speaking from the heart.

00:15:39.440 --> 00:15:41.664
You capture the essence
of what it is you bring

00:15:41.664 --> 00:15:44.831
and you inspire everybody who sees it.

00:15:48.299 --> 00:15:49.707
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:15:49.707 --> 00:15:52.746
I made this video because
Perennial Harvest asked me to.

00:15:52.746 --> 00:15:54.503
So if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:15:54.503 --> 00:15:55.908
that you want me to profile next,

00:15:55.908 --> 00:15:57.454
leave it down in the comments below,

00:15:57.454 --> 00:15:58.429
and I'll see what I can do.

00:15:58.429 --> 00:16:00.020
I'd also love to know what did Travis say

00:16:00.020 --> 00:16:01.353
that really resonated with you,

00:16:01.353 --> 00:16:03.326
what point really hit
home the hardest for you,

00:16:03.326 --> 00:16:04.935
what did you enjoy, what change

00:16:04.935 --> 00:16:08.168
or difference are you going
to make in your business

00:16:08.168 --> 00:16:10.023
or life after watching this video?

00:16:10.023 --> 00:16:12.361
Leave it in the comments, I'm
going to join in the discussion.

00:16:12.361 --> 00:16:14.106
Thank you again for watching.

00:16:14.106 --> 00:16:16.595
I believe in you, I hope you continue to

00:16:16.595 --> 00:16:19.855
believe in yourself and
whatever your one word is.

00:16:19.855 --> 00:16:21.355
I'll see you soon.

00:16:27.588 --> 00:16:29.270
- Fake it 'til you make it.

00:16:29.270 --> 00:16:30.755
I've heard this from a
couple of entrepreneurs.

00:16:30.755 --> 00:16:33.371
Fake it 'til you make it is bullshit.

00:16:33.371 --> 00:16:35.304
If you fake it 'til you make it,

00:16:35.304 --> 00:16:37.574
that means you're living a lie.

00:16:37.574 --> 00:16:39.414
And if you live a lie long enough,

00:16:39.414 --> 00:16:41.207
you're going to be in pain.

00:16:41.207 --> 00:16:44.099
And you're going to start
to sleep 14 hours a night,

00:16:44.099 --> 00:16:46.262
because you can't wake up.

00:16:46.262 --> 00:16:50.747
So faking it 'til you make it
is not a long-term strategy.

00:16:50.747 --> 00:16:54.207
Fake it 'til you make it
is an insecure strategy.

00:16:54.207 --> 00:16:56.171
And again, that goes back to fear.

00:16:56.171 --> 00:16:58.400
If you're afraid, go hustle.

00:16:58.400 --> 00:17:00.748
It'll solve your problems,
but don't fake it.

00:17:00.748 --> 00:17:04.883
The way of the entrepreneur
is actually about

00:17:04.883 --> 00:17:08.383
looking at the world and finding problems.

00:17:10.893 --> 00:17:13.879
It's actually, some people
think that's a negative mindset,

00:17:13.879 --> 00:17:17.696
but actually I find
it's a curious mindset.

00:17:17.696 --> 00:17:19.881
It's you're out there
looking for problems,

00:17:19.881 --> 00:17:22.008
looking for things that aren't the way

00:17:22.008 --> 00:17:24.016
they should be or could be.

00:17:24.016 --> 00:17:24.849
Okay, you got a good idea.

00:17:24.849 --> 00:17:26.236
Look, everybody's got a good idea.

00:17:26.236 --> 00:17:27.693
- [Interviewer] Yeah.

00:17:27.693 --> 00:17:30.193
- Okay, everybody has an idea.

00:17:32.084 --> 00:17:33.413
Not everybody has a good idea,

00:17:33.413 --> 00:17:34.680
but lots of people have good ideas.

00:17:34.680 --> 00:17:36.398
Look, Uber's a good idea,

00:17:36.398 --> 00:17:38.480
you push a button and you get a ride.

00:17:38.480 --> 00:17:39.812
I don't know if we were the first person

00:17:39.812 --> 00:17:41.344
to ever think of that idea,

00:17:41.344 --> 00:17:44.511
but we were the first person to do it.

00:17:45.444 --> 00:17:48.527
And so you got to just go do it, then.

00:17:50.092 --> 00:17:52.490
You got to just get
out there and go do it.

00:17:52.490 --> 00:17:55.455
So it's easy to have the
idea, straightforward.

00:17:55.455 --> 00:17:59.622
But then you got to have enough
guts to go out there and try.

00:18:00.928 --> 00:18:03.486
And that's the way of the entrepreneur.

